WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:05.669
Hubble has been the most productive
telescope in history but its successor

00:00:05.669 --> 00:00:10.789
the James Webb Space Telescope is
expected to surpass it in many ways--

00:00:10.840 --> 00:00:15.320
looking further back to when the
 universe 's first stars and galaxies formed.

00:00:15.320 --> 00:00:21.560
And probing the atmospheres of planets around other
stars for signs of life.

00:00:21.570 --> 00:00:26.210
To meet these goals NASA and its
partners are building the biggest. most

00:00:26.210 --> 00:00:30.630
complex, and most expensive science
mission ever attempted.

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:34.879
In the process engineers 
have pushed technology to the limit

00:00:34.879 --> 00:00:40.260
--lighter and stronger mirrors, made of the
metal beryllium coated with gold for

00:00:40.260 --> 00:00:43.180
superior reflection of infrared light

00:00:43.460 --> 00:00:47.180
--heat insulation from a sunshield 
the size of a tennis court

00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:53.700
and instruments that must work at a chilly
 230 degrees C below freezing.

00:00:53.880 --> 00:00:56.840
And its so gigantic, that for launch

00:00:56.860 --> 00:01:01.810
the entire telescope must fold up 
to fit inside the rocket.

00:01:01.810 --> 00:01:05.350
Once launched, Webb will set off on a
thirty-day

00:01:05.350 --> 00:01:07.130
1-million-mile journey

00:01:07.130 --> 00:01:11.930
to its final orbital position, all the
while carefully unfolding itself.

00:01:11.980 --> 00:01:17.820
First, the solar array unfolds to power the
telescope. Then the communication dishes

00:01:17.829 --> 00:01:23.479
move into place. Next, the sunshield opens
protecting the delicate instruments from

00:01:23.479 --> 00:01:27.139
the heat of the Sun.

00:01:28.540 --> 00:01:34.340
Booms flip over revealing the secondary
mirror. And finally, the side tiles pop

00:01:34.340 --> 00:01:37.190
out to complete the primary mirror.

00:01:37.190 --> 00:01:41.310
Scientists will spend many weeks waiting
for the telescope to cool down, while

00:01:41.310 --> 00:01:46.770
fine-tuning the position and shape of
each of the 18 tiles in the main mirror.

00:01:46.770 --> 00:01:51.539
Only then, after two months in space will
the world's biggest eye in the sky

00:01:51.539 --> 00:01:55.079
turn its gaze on the cosmos.

